# RFC-0011: Webhooks

Status: Active

## Abstract

A webhook event is declared by putting `[WebhookEvent("resource.action")]` on a
command. The Mediator pipeline publishes it after the command succeeds, and
deliveries are signed with HMAC-SHA256 and retried in the background.

## 1. Declaring an Event

- Annotate the outer command class (or the nested `Command` record) with
  `[WebhookEvent("resource.action")]`, optionally with `DisplayName` and `Group`
  for the admin event picker.
- Event names MUST match `^[a-z][a-z0-9_]*\.[a-z][a-z0-9_]*$` —
  `content_item.created`, `user.created` — and MUST be unique across the
  codebase. `ApplicationConventionTests` enforces both, plus that an annotated
  type declares a nested Mediator `Command`.
- An event name is a published contract for receivers. Renaming one is a
  breaking change; add a new event instead.
- `WebhookEventCatalog` discovers events by reflection. Do not maintain a second
  hand-written list, and do not register an event that no command raises.
- Only annotate commands. Queries MUST NOT fire webhooks.

## 2. Publishing

- `WebhookPublishBehavior` runs after the handler and publishes only when the
  response reports success. A failed command MUST NOT emit an event.
- `WebhookPublishBehavior` always publishes after a successful annotated command.
- A publish failure is logged and swallowed — it MUST NOT fail the command that
  triggered it. Do not "fix" that by rethrowing.
- The payload is `{ result, request, requestType }`, with the request serialized
  and any property whose name contains `password`, `secret`, `token`, `apikey`,
  or `api_key` stripped. New command properties that carry credentials MUST be
  named so that redaction catches them, or kept off the command entirely.
- Delivery is enqueued as a background task; publishing MUST NOT make an outbound
  HTTP call inline.

## 3. Signing

Every delivery is a `POST` of the JSON payload with:

| Header | Value |
|---|---|
| `X-Raytha-Signature` | `sha256=<lowercase hex>` — HMAC-SHA256 of the raw body, keyed by the webhook secret |
| `X-Raytha-Event` | the event name |
| `X-Raytha-Delivery` | the delivery id, for receiver-side idempotency |
| `X-Raytha-Timestamp` | attempt time, round-trip format |

- Signing MUST go through `WebhookSigner`. It signs the exact bytes that are
  sent, so changing serialization after signing breaks verification.
- Secrets are generated with `WebhookSigner.GenerateSecret()` (32 random bytes,
  hex) and returned once in `CreatedWebhookDto`. Never expose a secret again
  (RFC-0008 §7).
- Verification uses `WebhookSigner.Verify`, which is a fixed-time comparison.
  String equality on signatures is FORBIDDEN.

## 4. Delivery and Retry

- Each attempt is a `WebhookDelivery` row: attempt count, last attempt, next
  retry, response status, error. Delivery history is the operator's debugging
  surface — keep it accurate.
- `DeliverWebhookTask` retries with capped exponential backoff up to the
  webhook's `MaxAttempts`, honoring its `TimeoutSeconds`. Those are per-webhook
  settings; do not hardcode either.
- Requests go through the named `raytha-webhooks` `HttpClient`. New outbound
  webhook traffic MUST use it rather than constructing an `HttpClient`.
- Webhook URLs are operator-supplied and reach the network from the server. They
  are subject to the same internal-URL policy as other outbound fetches
  (RFC-0008 §6).
